This is how I define it:

Candidate Expected Value (EV)
The Candidate Expected Value (EV) model estimates the potential value of pursuing employment opportunities by combining hiring probability, career value, and retention probability.

\text{Candidate EV} = P(\text{Hire}) \times \text{Career Value} \times \text{Retention Probability}
Where:

P(Hire) = Probability of successfully obtaining the position
Career Value = Estimated value of the role (salary, growth, experience, network effects, etc.)
Retention Probability = Probability of remaining employed long enough to realize the expected value
Interpretation
A position with a high salary but low hiring probability may have a lower expected value than a position with a moderate salary and significantly higher hiring probability.

This framework treats career decisions similarly to investment decisions by evaluating both risk and reward.
 
